    "font size" : 10.909,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Now, we prove that Ψc \\{(0,m) : m ≥ c} ⊂ Ψ∗c. For any 0 < p ≤ 1, consider any (p,m) ∈ Ψc and the following probability mass function for a random variable T:"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 4,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 211.251, 610.275, 400.748, 630.653 ],
    "font" : "NimbusRomNo9L-Regu",
    "font size" : 10.909,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Pr{T = 0} = 1 − p and Pr{T = m/p} = p."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 5,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 90.0, 578.992, 263.237, 599.37 ],
    "font" : "NimbusRomNo9L-Regu",
    "font size" : 10.909,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Since m ≥ cp, we have m/p ≥ c, and so"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 6,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 239.372, 547.709, 372.628, 568.087 ],
    "font" : "NimbusRomNo9L-ReguItal",
    "font size" : 10.909,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Pr{T ≥ c} = Pr{T = m/p} = p,"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 7,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 90.0, 503.336, 521.998, 536.805 ],
    "font" : "NimbusRomNo9L-Regu",
    "font size" : 10.909,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "and E[T] = p(m/p) = m. Therefore, (p,m) is achieved by the random variable T as constructed. Note that unless m = cp, (p,m) can always be achieved by more than one probability distribution."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 8,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 90.0, 442.365, 522.005, 496.157 ],
    "font" : "NimbusRomNo9L-Regu",
    "font size" : 10.909,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "It remains to prove that every ordered pair (0,m) with 0 ≤ m < c is achievable. This can be done by noting that such an ordered pair can be achieved by any random variable T with Pr{T = m} = 1. The theorem is proved. □"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 9,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 90.0, 349.114, 522.004, 423.231 ],
    "font" : "NimbusRomNo9L-Regu",
    "font size" : 10.909,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "The region Ψc is defined by Markov’s inequality together with the constraint 0 ≤ p ≤ 1 which comes from the setup of the problem, and we have shown that for any fixed c > 0, every ordered pair in Ψc except for a region with Lebesgue measure 0 (namely the region {(0,m) : m ≥ c}) is achievable by some random variable T. Specifically:"
  }, {
    "type" : "list",
    "pdfua_tag" : "L",
